# ── Secrets / local config ───────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.*
!.env.template
*.key
*.pem

# GCP credentials for VOD reprocessing (see REPROCESS_VOD.md)
.gcp-adc.json
.gcp-*.json
gcp-*.json

# ── Per-deployment runtime state ─────────────────────────────────────────
# These files are seeded empty in the repo; the app writes user data into
# them at runtime. Don't track those edits.
outputs.json
vod-jobs.json
# glossary.json is TRACKED again as of 2026-09-01. It was ignored because this
# repo was public and the file names the event; both repos went private that
# day, which removes the reason. Tracking it is now the point: the glossary is
# the whole quality margin (a briefed model scores 24/24 on terminology, the
# same model cold scores like the cheap one), it was surviving only as an
# untracked file on one Windows PC, and it is the artefact a Gujarati reader
# reviews. An unbacked-up file that nobody can review is the bigger risk.
# glossary.example.json stays as the empty schema for a fresh deployment.
#
# Still true, and unchanged by any of this: NO KEYS IN TRACKED FILES. Private
# is not a reason to relax .env.

# Operator logs. A deployment that redirects server output into the repo
# leaves `?? logs/` in git status — the same invisible trap as the
# glossary, and the mandir PC walked into it within the hour. A caption
# log holds the whole katha transcript, so committing one to a public
# repo is worse than the glossary would have been.
logs/
*.log

# ── Generated / cached / large ───────────────────────────────────────────
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# Frontend
web/node_modules/
web/dist/
web/.vite/
*.tsbuildinfo

# ── Local artefacts (recordings, uploads, samples) ───────────────────────
results/
uploads/
samples/
screenshots/
# (samples/ also gitignored — drop your own test audio in there locally.)

